      "role": "user",
      "content": "I see. So it really comes down to the concept of foreseeability and control. What about scenarios where the disruption feels almost... malicious or intentional in its disruption of the market, but there isn't a single clear perpetrator? Like, if there's just a widespread, unexplained period of social or economic chaos that prevents people from reaching the retail area? How does the court view that kind of fundamental disruption to the \"order\" of business?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When the disruption is widespread, chaotic, and lacks a single identifiable perpetrator—like a general societal breakdown or pervasive economic instability—the court starts to look at the *effect* on the contract's core purpose. This is where the concept of \"frustration of purpose\" can become relevant, even if the clause doesn't explicitly mention it.\n\nIf the chaos fundamentally alters the basis upon which the lease was agreed—meaning the entire premise of a functioning retail market in that area is gone—it moves closer to a legitimate invocation of force majeure, provided the clause is broad enough. The court will assess whether the disruption is so pervasive that it renders the *entire commercial venture* impossible, not just temporarily difficult.\n\nHowever, these \"soft\" disruptions are the hardest to prove, as courts are wary of allowing tenants to escape obligations simply because business is tough. You must demonstrate that the chaos is an external, overwhelming force that prevents the *use* of the premises as intended.\n\n*It is crucial to have a legal expert review the specific language regarding \"market disruption\" or \"social unrest\" in your agreement.*",
